{"explanation":"Apply the selector with threshold equal to the maximum of $X$ and $D$ times the product of the supplied history. Define $x_0=1$ and let each later $x$ value be the ceiling logarithm of the corresponding denominator. The selector's first inequalities give the approximation, positivity, and $X$ margin. The product identity for normalized logarithmic weights converts the history product in the selector condition into $\\prod_{j<i}x_j$, yielding the strict product growth. No sign assumptions on $X$ or $D$ are needed for this implication.","natural_language_statement":"Let $\\nu,X,D\\in\\mathbb R$. Suppose that for every threshold rule $T$ on finite lists there are integer numerators $p_n$ and natural denominators $q_n$ such that, for every $n$, $q_n\\ge2$, $p_n\\ne0$, $|\\pi-p_n/q_n|\\le q_n^{-\\nu}$, and the logarithmic ceiling weights satisfy $1\\le\\lceil\\log q_n\\rceil$ and exceed $T$ applied to the earlier weights in reverse order. Then there are sequences $p_n,q_n,x_n$ with $x_0=1$ and $x_{n+1}=\\lceil\\log q_n\\rceil$ for which\n\n$$x_i\\ge1,\\quad X<x_{n+1},\\quad D\\prod_{j<i}x_j<x_i\\quad(i>0),$$\n\nwhile every pair $(p_n,q_n)$ retains the same power-law approximation, nonzero-numerator, denominator, and positive-weight conditions. The selector hypothesis is part of the result. This normalization packages the uniform lower margin and multiplicative separation into a sequence that can be reused when later admissibility constraints depend on accumulated weights. No sign restrictions on $X$ or $D$ are required.","source_witness":{"inline_helper_ast_witness":{"ast_command_sha256":"1e331b5db8e8d7c94cfe3b942e30ee8a0acfd6c64242ab48851ce40376a52f07","generated_companion":false,"generated_name":"OAI.PiExponent.normalizedLogWeights_zero","graph_kind":"theorem","no_companion_identity_inferred":true,"raw_command_sha256":"113c21a2e6f1dbd0e345f24f49e26eba17e39b1be8681e82f67024629a8e0942","raw_command_utf8":"@[simp] theorem normalizedLogWeights_zero (q : ℕ →